{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 32, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "the social network is a film that masterfully weaves together the story of facebook's creation and the tumultuous relationships that surrounded it. directed by david fincher, the movie is a gripping and thought-provoking exploration of the complexities of human connection in the digital age.\n\nthe film's narrative is presented in a non-linear fashion, jumping back and forth between different periods in the life of mark zuckerberg, the founder of facebook. we see him as a sophomore at harvard, struggling to navigate the complexities of social relationships and seeking to create a website that will help him connect with others. we also see him as a successful entrepreneur, facing lawsuits and personal crises as his creation becomes a global phenomenon.\n\nthe cast delivers strong performances throughout, with jesse eisenberg bringing a nuanced and multifaceted portrayal to the role of mark zuckerberg. his performance captures the character's awkwardness and intensity, as well as his intelligence and drive. andrew garfield and justin timberlake also shine as eduardo saverin and sean parker, respectively, bringing depth and complexity to their characters.\n\none of the most striking aspects of the film is its exploration of the themes of friendship, betrayal, and the consequences of our actions. as mark's creation becomes increasingly successful, he finds himself at odds with his friends and colleagues, and the film raises important questions about the costs of ambition and the nature of personal relationships in the digital age.\n\nthe film's visuals are also noteworthy, with a muted color palette and a keen attention to detail that helps to bring the world of harvard and silicon valley to life. the score, composed by trent reznor and atticus ross, is equally impressive, adding a layer of tension and unease to the film's narrative.\n\novera